title: Azure Policy definitions, hands-on
guide: howto/azure-policy.html
promise: Azure Policy checks changes to your Azure resources against rules you write: learn its definition language by building policies that fail quietly in a throwaway resource group, then fixing them.

== Who it is for and what you need
section: How to use this guide
Readers who have run Azure CLI (command-line interface) commands and can read JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) should allow about two hours.

You need a subscription you may experiment in, the Azure CLI and jq, a command-line tool for reading JSON.

== How the guide was checked
section: How to use this guide
No az (Azure CLI) walk-through command ran against Azure: expected results are what Microsoft's documentation says; yours may differ.

Rules were validated against Microsoft's schema; shell blocks ran in bash and zsh with a stand-in az, to catch quoting errors.

== How to follow the guide
section: How to use this guide
Read it in order. Paste BASH blocks into one terminal, since later steps reuse step 2's SUB variable. Paste each cat > file <<'EOF' block whole, EOF line included. JSON blocks are to read; the walk-through writes its own files.

== Set up: check the subscription
section: 2. Choose the subscription and make the lab
In the subscription you must be able to create policy definitions and assign roles on one resource group; Owner covers both. Azure Policy has no charge on Azure resources. Check that this prints the subscription you mean to use.
```bash
az account show --query "{name:name, id:id}" -o table
```

== Set up: make the lab resource group
section: 2. Choose the subscription and make the lab
Step 2 keeps the subscription ID in SUB, creates this group and adds lab-nsg-before, a network security group: the firewall rules for an Azure virtual network. The lab creates only network security groups, which run nothing. Step 8 removes everything.
```bash
az group create --name csoh-policy-lab --location eastus --tags owner=platform-team
```

== What Azure Policy is and where you meet it
section: A rule engine in front of every change
Every create, update and delete in Azure passes through Azure Resource Manager, and Azure Policy is the rule engine inside that path. Platform and cloud security teams write policy to set guardrails that hold whichever tool a team deploys with.

== A definition does nothing until it is assigned
section: A rule engine in front of every change
A policy definition pairs a condition with an effect, such as deny, which fails the request, or audit, which marks the resource non-compliant and lets the request through. An assignment applies it to a scope, such as a resource group.

== A policy acts only on what it can see
section: Five ways a policy silently does nothing
A comparison against an absent property is false, so the effect does not apply and the resource is silently reported compliant. The deliberate exceptions, exists: false and a not (negation) around a positive test, are true for an absent property.

== The first attempt: conditions, then one effect
section: Anatomy of a definition
Step 3 saves the first attempt as ssh-naive.json, meant to deny rules letting SSH (Secure Shell) in from anywhere. These are its last allOf (and) conditions. A long field name is an alias for a path, such as properties.destinationPortRange.
```json
          { "field": "Microsoft.Network/networkSecurityGroups/securityRules/destinationPortRange", "equals": "22" },
          { "field": "Microsoft.Network/networkSecurityGroups/securityRules/sourceAddressPrefix", "in": ["*", "Internet", "0.0.0.0/0"] }
        ]
      },
      "then": { "effect": "deny" }
```

== The corrected rule counts the list form
section: Arrays and count
Rules carry ports as destinationPortRange or the list destinationPortRanges, never both. Step 4's ssh.json checks both; for the list it uses a count, which evaluates its where condition per member and compares how many matched. Its effect is a parameter.
```json
  "count": {
    "field": "Microsoft.Network/networkSecurityGroups/securityRules/destinationPortRanges[*]",
    "where": {
      "field": "Microsoft.Network/networkSecurityGroups/securityRules/destinationPortRanges[*]",
      "in": ["22", "*"]
    }
  },
  "greater": 0
```

== The mode decides what is evaluated at all
section: 4. Write the corrected rule, and create it twice
Step 4 creates csoh-lab-ssh from ssh.json with --mode from the file, All, then csoh-lab-ssh-indexed like this, without one. That one gets Indexed, which evaluates only resource types that support tags and location: never a network security group's rules.
```bash
az policy definition create --name csoh-lab-ssh-indexed --params "$(jq -c .properties.parameters ssh.json)" --rules "$(jq -c .properties.policyRule ssh.json)"
```

== Four assignments carry an SSH rule
section: 5. Assign everything to the lab, and test
Step 5 assigns the first attempt as lab-ssh-naive, a deny, and the corrected rule three times: lab-ssh-indexed, a deny in Indexed mode; lab-ssh-audit, an audit; and this trial deny in DoNotEnforce mode, which reports but never applies the effect.
```bash
az policy assignment create --name lab-ssh-trial --policy csoh-lab-ssh --resource-group csoh-policy-lab --params '{"effect": {"value": "Deny"}}' --enforcement-mode DoNotEnforce
```

== SSH from anywhere on two ports should get past all four
section: 5. Assign everything to the lab, and test
Assignments take about five minutes to apply. Expect it created: the CLI sends two ports only as destinationPortRanges. Scan for compliance with az policy state trigger-scan; az policy state list should show lab-ssh-naive Compliant, lab-ssh-audit and lab-ssh-trial NonCompliant, lab-ssh-indexed absent.
```bash
az network nsg rule create --resource-group csoh-policy-lab --nsg-name lab-nsg-before --name allow-ssh-https --priority 110 --direction Inbound --access Allow --protocol Tcp --source-address-prefixes '*' --destination-port-ranges 22 443 --query "{name:name, destinationPortRange:destinationPortRange, destinationPortRanges:destinationPortRanges}"
```

== Enforce the trial; existing resources stay
section: 6. Enforce the corrected rule
Switched to Default, which applies the effect, lab-ssh-trial should deny a new rule for ports 22 and 3389 after a few minutes. allow-ssh-https is still there: a deny stops new requests and leaves existing resources as they are.
```bash
az policy assignment update --name lab-ssh-trial --resource-group csoh-policy-lab --enforcement-mode Default
```

== Remediate what already existed
section: 7. Remediate the existing resource
Step 4's inherit-owner.json, a modify policy, copies the group's owner tag onto resources lacking one. Step 5 assigns it with a managed identity holding Tag Contributor, a role for managing tags. This remediation task should tag lab-nsg-before, which existed first.
```bash
az policy remediation create --name lab-fix-owner --policy-assignment lab-inherit-owner --resource-group csoh-policy-lab
```

== Exercise: a colleague "fixes" the first attempt
section: Hands-on exercises
The colleague replaces its port condition with this one. Which would the new policy deny: allow-ssh (port 22), allow-ssh-https (22 and 443), and a rule allowing only port 80 from anywhere? Check it in the lab before step 8.
```json
{
  "field": "Microsoft.Network/networkSecurityGroups/securityRules/destinationPortRanges[*]",
  "equals": "22"
}
```

== Answer: wrong in both directions
section: Hands-on exercises
It denies allow-ssh and the port 80 rule but allows allow-ssh-https. A field condition on a [*] alias holds only when every member satisfies it: 443 is not 22, and one-port rules have no list, so no member breaks it.

== Exercise: an empty activity log
section: Hands-on exercises
A deny assignment for "no SSH from the internet" has been in place for a month, and a colleague cites the empty activity log, no denials at all, as evidence that nobody has tried. What do you check?

== Answer: four checks before you trust the log
section: Hands-on exercises
Check the assignment's enforcementMode, since under DoNotEnforce nothing is denied or logged; the compliance results, recorded either way; the definition's mode, as Indexed never evaluates network security group rules; and the condition, against a test resource in each property form.

== Common mistakes
section: Common mistakes
- A definition created without a mode is Indexed.
- A [*] field condition means every member; use count.
- Audit stops nothing; neither does a trial left in DoNotEnforce.
- Without --resource-group or --scope, az policy assignment create assigns at the whole subscription.
